Q: Splitting a string into tokens with several possible separators, using `std::ranges` A: <p>Warning: this isn't a correct answer to the question precisely as asked--this requires C++23, not just C++20.</p>
<p>C++23 adds a new <code>view::chunk_by</code> that uses a predicate to determine where to break an input range into tokens. Our predicate receives two successive items in the input range, and returns <code>true</code> if they should be part of the same token, or <code>false</code> if the second should be in a separate token from the first.</p>
<p>That lets us break the input up into tokens consisting solely of whitespace, and tokens consisting solely of non-whitespace. From there we need to filter them to get only the non-whitespace tokens.</p>
<pre class="lang-cpp prettyprint-override"><code>#include &lt;iostream&gt;
#include &lt;string_view&gt;
#include &lt;ranges&gt;
#include &lt;cctype&gt;

// Support printing the subrange objects created by chunk_by:
std::ostream &amp;operator&lt;&lt;(std::ostream &amp;os, 
    std::ranges::subrange&lt;char const*, char const*, std::ranges::subrange_kind::sized&gt; &amp;s) 
{
    return os &lt;&lt; std::string_view(s.begin(), s.end());
}

int main() {
    std::string_view line{&quot;\tSplit   \t\t   this sequence\t of   tokens  &quot;};
    auto words = line | 
        std::views::chunk_by([](char a, char b) { return !std::isspace(a) == !std::isspace(b); }) | 
        std::views::filter([](auto s) { return !std::isspace(s[0]); });

    for (auto word : words)
    {
        std::cout &lt;&lt; &quot;[&quot; &lt;&lt; word &lt;&lt; &quot;]&quot;;
    }
    std::cout &lt;&lt; &quot;\n&quot;;
}
</code></pre>
<p>Result:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>[Split][this][sequence][of][tokens]</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Notes:</p>
<ul>
<li><p>In the predicate to chunk_by, we negate the results of both calls to <code>isspace</code>, to convert them to <code>bool</code>. <code>isspace</code> is only required to return 0 or non-zero, not necessarily 0 or 1/true, so (in theory) one call could return 1 and the other call 2, even though it's indicating true in both cases.</p>
</li>
<li><p>For the moment this assumes input characters will be ASCII. If we care about processing international characters, we should cast to <code>unsigned char</code> before we call <code>issspace</code>.</p>
</li>
</ul>

